-- | OpenZeppelin Timelock - a mandatory delay between the proposal of a
-- privileged operation and its execution.
--
-- The Daml analogue of `TimelockController.sol`, built on two interfaces. The
-- protected contract implements `Timelock`: its view carries the delay policy
-- and the list of pending operations, and its `apply` method applies one
-- operation's typed parameters. Each operation kind is a template of the
-- consumer's own, with the typed parameters as fields, that implements
-- `Operation` and `Timelocked`. The interfaces carry the lifecycle choices:
-- `Operation_Execute`, `Operation_Cancel`, and `Operation_Cleanup` on the
-- operation, and `Timelock_Apply` and `Timelock_Drop` on the protected
-- contract. The consumer writes the schedule choice, which creates the
-- operation and records it as pending, and the `apply` method, which
-- dispatches on the operation's template with `fromInterface`.
--
-- # Authority model
--
-- The protected contract's signatories authorize both ends of the flow. The
-- schedule choice runs on the protected contract, so the operation it creates
-- carries those signatures, and the same choice records the operation in the
-- protected contract's `Pending` list. `Operation_Execute` runs on the
-- operation with the executor as controller; its body carries the operation's
-- signatures, which is what lets it exercise `Timelock_Apply`, whose
-- controller is the protected contract's `authority`. `Timelock_Apply` takes
-- the operation out of the pending list with `takePending`, calls the
-- consumer's `apply`, and checks that the successor dropped the operation.
-- The list binds the delay on the signatories themselves: an operation a
-- signatory creates directly is absent from the list and is refused, and the
-- schedule choice is the only way in.
--
-- Executors and cancellers are named on the operation's view, and the
-- schedule choice's controller is the proposer. A fixed party, a party set, an
-- open executor, or a role credential checked in the schedule choice all fit.
--
-- # Time on Canton
--
-- A Daml transaction carries a ledger time that Canton checks against the
-- record time within a tolerance that the synchronizer configures. The guards
-- use the ledger-time bound primitives `isLedgerTimeGE` and `isLedgerTimeLT`,
-- so each check constrains one bound and leaves the ledger time free within
-- it. A submitter chooses its ledger time anywhere inside the tolerance, so a
-- proposer using `scheduleAt` shortens the effective delay by up to the whole
-- tolerance, one minute by default. `scheduleAfter` reads `getTime`, which
-- fixes the ledger time and keeps the delay exact. Choose a delay far longer
-- than the tolerance: minutes at least, and days for governance.
--
-- Execution is a submission. Once an operation is ready, an executor submits
-- the apply choice; an automation that watches the interface view and submits
-- at `readyAt` is the consumer's off-ledger component, as in the Canton
-- Network's own governance.
--
-- # Scope
--
-- One operation is one contract and one apply choice. A Daml choice body is
-- atomic, so a batch is one operation template whose parameters list several
-- effects, applied in one choice. Ordering between operations is a
-- precondition on the protected contract's state, which is a stronger check
-- than the identity of an earlier operation.
--
-- # Package boundary
--
-- This package holds the frozen interface, the schedule functions, and the
-- guards. Operation templates live in consuming packages, where they upgrade
-- through Smart Contract Upgrade independently of this package.
module OpenZeppelin.TimelockV1 where

import DA.Action (unless, when)
import DA.Fail (FailureCategory (..), FailureStatus (..), failWithStatus)
import DA.List (delete)
import DA.Optional (whenSome)
import qualified DA.TextMap as TextMap
import DA.Time (RelTime, addRelTime, isLedgerTimeGE, isLedgerTimeLE, isLedgerTimeLT, microseconds)

-- | When a scheduled operation may execute.
data TimelockedView = TimelockedView with
    readyAt : Time
      -- ^ The earliest ledger time at which the operation may execute.
    expiresAt : Optional Time
      -- ^ The ledger time from which the operation may no longer execute, or
      --   `None` when the operation stays executable until it is archived.
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- | A scheduled operation. Implement this on every template that represents an
-- operation waiting out its delay, whatever authority proposed it.
--
-- Signatories and observers: those of the implementing template. An operation
--   created in a choice on the protected contract carries that contract's
--   signatures.
-- Controllers: the consumer's choices name them. The interface is view-only,
--   because an interface choice needs a controller drawn from the view and the
--   view carries times, not parties.
-- Choices: scheduling, applying, cancelling, and cleaning up are choices on
--   the consumer's protected and operation templates.
-- Disclosure and privacy expectations: a pending operation is visible to the
--   stakeholders of the implementing template. An executor outside that set
--   receives the contract by explicit disclosure. Keep sensitive parameters off
--   a template that many parties observe.
-- Authorization assumptions: scheduling and applying use the protected
--   contract's signatory authority, which choices on that contract carry.
-- Archival behavior: the consumer's apply choice archives the operation when
--   it executes, the cancel choice archives it early, and a cleanup choice
--   archives it after `expiresAt`.
-- Failure modes: the guards raise `eNotReady`, `eExpired`, and `eNotExpired`;
--   the schedule functions raise `eInvalidConfig` and `eDelayTooShort`.
-- Upgrade and migration assumptions: frozen for the life of the package.
--   `TimelockedView` keeps its two fields and this interface keeps zero
--   methods. A breaking change ships as a sibling `-v2` package.
interface Timelocked where
  viewtype TimelockedView

-- | The operations a protected contract has scheduled and may still apply,
-- cancel, or clean up. Hold it as a field of the protected template beside
-- `TimelockConfig` and expose both through `TimelockView`. The schedule choice
-- appends with `addPending`; `Timelock_Apply` and `Timelock_Drop` remove with
-- `takePending`, which refuses an operation the list does not hold. Every
-- choice that changes the list is consuming, so a protected contract with
-- pending operations changes its contract id on each schedule and each apply.
type Pending = [ContractId Timelocked]

-- | Record a newly created operation in the protected contract's list. Call it
-- in the schedule choice on the operation it created:
--
-- > op <- create LimitChange with ..
-- > create this with pending = addPending op pending
addPending : (HasToInterface t Timelocked) => ContractId t -> Pending -> Pending
addPending op pending = toInterfaceContractId @Timelocked op :: pending

-- | Remove an operation from the protected contract's list, and fail with
-- `eNotPending` when the list does not hold it. `Timelock_Apply` and
-- `Timelock_Drop` call it, so only operations the schedule choice created reach
-- an effect. A consumer that writes its own apply choice calls it first.
takePending : (HasToInterface t Timelocked) => ContractId t -> Pending -> Update Pending
takePending op pending = do
  let cid = toInterfaceContractId @Timelocked op
  unless (cid `elem` pending) (failWithStatus eNotPending)
  pure (delete cid pending)

-- | The delay policy of a protected contract. Hold it as a field of the
-- template whose privileged choices the timelock guards, so that the policy is
-- part of the state that the schedule choice already exercises.
--
-- Changing the policy is itself a privileged operation. A consumer that wants
-- the `updateDelay` behavior of `TimelockController.sol` schedules the change
-- as an operation under the current policy, which is the self-administration
-- pattern the example package shows.
data TimelockConfig = TimelockConfig with
    minDelay : RelTime
      -- ^ The shortest delay a schedule may request, zero or greater. Zero
      --   disables the delay.
    gracePeriod : Optional RelTime
      -- ^ How long after `readyAt` an operation stays executable. `None`
      --   keeps an operation executable until it is archived, matching
      --   `TimelockController.sol`. When set, a positive duration: it is the
      --   width of the window in which `requireReady` succeeds.
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- | Whether a policy produces executable operations: `minDelay` is zero or
-- greater, and `gracePeriod`, when set, is positive. The schedule functions
-- check it, which keeps a protected template able to schedule operations
-- after its policy changes through a timelocked operation.
isValidConfig : TimelockConfig -> Bool
isValidConfig config =
  config.minDelay >= aeternity && optional True (> aeternity) config.gracePeriod
  where aeternity = microseconds 0

-- | Fail with `eInvalidConfig` unless `isValidConfig` holds. The schedule
-- functions call it on the policy in force. Call it on the proposed policy in
-- the choice that schedules a policy change, so that an invalid policy is
-- refused at proposal time.
requireValidConfig : TimelockConfig -> Update ()
requireValidConfig config = unless (isValidConfig config) (failWithStatus eInvalidConfig)

-- | Compute the schedule of an operation that becomes ready at `readyAt`.
-- Call it in the choice that creates the operation contract and store the
-- returned fields on it:
--
-- > choice Treasury_ScheduleLimit : (ContractId Treasury, ContractId LimitChange)
-- >   with newLimit : Decimal; readyAt : Time
-- >   controller proposer
-- >   do
-- >     s <- scheduleAt config readyAt
-- >     op <- create LimitChange with
-- >       admin; proposer; executor; newLimit
-- >       readyAt = s.readyAt; expiresAt = s.expiresAt
-- >     t <- create this with pending = addPending op pending
-- >     pure (t, op)
--
-- Fails with `eInvalidConfig` when `isValidConfig` rejects the policy, and
-- with `eDelayTooShort` unless the ledger time is at least `config.minDelay`
-- before `readyAt`. The check is a ledger-time bound, so the transaction's
-- ledger time stays free within it. Record the created operation with
-- `addPending`.
scheduleAt : TimelockConfig -> Time -> Update TimelockedView
scheduleAt config readyAt = do
  requireValidConfig config
  early <- isLedgerTimeLE (addRelTime readyAt (negate config.minDelay))
  unless early (failWithStatus eDelayTooShort)
  pure TimelockedView with
    readyAt
    expiresAt = fmap (addRelTime readyAt) config.gracePeriod

-- | Compute the schedule of an operation that becomes ready `delay` after the
-- transaction's ledger time. It reads `getTime`, which fixes the ledger time
-- of the transaction; use `scheduleAt` when the proposer knows the target time.
--
-- Fails with `eInvalidConfig` when `isValidConfig` rejects the policy, and
-- with `eDelayTooShort` unless `delay` is at least `config.minDelay`.
scheduleAfter : TimelockConfig -> RelTime -> Update TimelockedView
scheduleAfter config delay = do
  requireValidConfig config
  unless (delay >= config.minDelay) (failWithStatus eDelayTooShort)
  now <- getTime
  let readyAt = addRelTime now delay
  pure TimelockedView with
    readyAt
    expiresAt = fmap (addRelTime readyAt) config.gracePeriod

-- | Fail unless the operation may execute now: the ledger time is at or after
-- `readyAt` and, when `expiresAt` is set, before it. Call it in the apply
-- choice after `takePending` and before applying the parameters:
--
-- > choice Treasury_ApplyLimit : ContractId Treasury
-- >   with opCid : ContractId LimitChange
-- >   controller executor
-- >   do
-- >     pending' <- takePending opCid pending
-- >     op <- fetch opCid
-- >     requireReady op
-- >     archive opCid
-- >     create this with limit = op.newLimit, pending = pending'
--
-- Accepts an implementing template or a `Timelocked`. Fails with `eNotReady`
-- before `readyAt` and with `eExpired` at or after `expiresAt`.
requireReady : (HasToInterface t Timelocked) => t -> Update ()
requireReady x = do
  let v = view (toInterface @Timelocked x)
  ready <- isLedgerTimeGE v.readyAt
  unless ready (failWithStatus eNotReady)
  whenSome v.expiresAt $ \expiresAt -> do
    live <- isLedgerTimeLT expiresAt
    unless live (failWithStatus eExpired)

-- | Fail unless the operation has expired: `expiresAt` is set and the ledger
-- time is at or after it. Use it on a cleanup choice that lets any party
-- archive an expired operation.
--
-- Fails with `eNotExpired` when `expiresAt` is `None` or lies in the future.
requireExpired : (HasToInterface t Timelocked) => t -> Update ()
requireExpired x = do
  let v = view (toInterface @Timelocked x)
  case v.expiresAt of
    None -> failWithStatus eNotExpired
    Some expiresAt -> do
      expired <- isLedgerTimeGE expiresAt
      unless expired (failWithStatus eNotExpired)

-- | Whether the operation may execute at `now`: `readyAt <= now`, and
-- `now < expiresAt` when `expiresAt` is set. A pure function for a choice or
-- an off-ledger reader that branches rather than refuses.
isReadyAt : (HasToInterface t Timelocked) => Time -> t -> Bool
isReadyAt now x =
  let v = view (toInterface @Timelocked x)
  in v.readyAt <= now && optional True (now <) v.expiresAt

-- | Whether the operation has expired at `now`: `expiresAt` is set and
-- `expiresAt <= now`.
isExpiredAt : (HasToInterface t Timelocked) => Time -> t -> Bool
isExpiredAt now x =
  let v = view (toInterface @Timelocked x)
  in optional False (<= now) v.expiresAt

-- | The state of a protected contract that the lifecycle choices read.
data TimelockView = TimelockView with
    authority : Party
      -- ^ The signatory whose authority applies operations. `Timelock_Apply`
      --   and `Timelock_Drop` name it as controller; an operation created in a
      --   choice on the protected contract carries its signature.
    config : TimelockConfig
      -- ^ The delay policy in force.
    pending : Pending
      -- ^ The operations the schedule choice created and that may still
      --   apply, cancel, or clean up.
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- | Who may execute and cancel an operation.
data OperationView = OperationView with
    executors : Optional [Party]
      -- ^ The parties that may call `Operation_Execute`. `None` opens
      --   execution to any party, the `address(0)` executor of
      --   `TimelockController.sol`; an open executor receives the operation
      --   and the protected contract by explicit disclosure.
    cancellers : [Party]
      -- ^ The parties that may call `Operation_Cancel`.
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- | The protected contract: the template whose privileged state changes only
-- through matured operations. Implement it beside a `TimelockConfig` field, a
-- `Pending` field, and the schedule choices you write.
--
-- Signatories and observers: those of the implementing template. Executors
--   and cancellers fetch the protected contract when they exercise the
--   operation's choices, so they observe it or receive it by disclosure.
-- Controllers: `authority` controls `Timelock_Apply` and `Timelock_Drop`.
--   Both are exercised from the operation's choices, whose bodies carry the
--   operation's signatures.
-- Choices: `Timelock_Apply` applies one pending operation through `apply`;
--   `Timelock_Drop` removes one pending operation through `unschedule`. Scheduling
--   is a choice the consumer writes, because it creates a typed operation.
-- Disclosure and privacy expectations: the view exposes the policy and the
--   pending list to every stakeholder of the protected contract. An open
--   executor sees the protected contract's payload at execution time.
-- Authorization assumptions: `authority` is a signatory of the implementing
--   template and of every operation its schedule choice creates; the pending
--   list holds only operations that choice created.
-- Archival behavior: both choices are consuming and the methods create the
--   successor, so the protected contract changes its contract id on each
--   apply and each drop, as it does on each schedule.
-- Failure modes: `eNotPending` for an operation outside the list;
--   `eOperationRetained` when `apply` or `unschedule` returns a successor that still
--   lists the operation.
-- Upgrade and migration assumptions: frozen for the life of the package. The
--   implementing template upgrades independently.
interface Timelock where
  viewtype TimelockView

  apply : Operation -> Pending -> Update (ContractId Timelock)
    -- ^ Apply one matured operation and create the successor. Dispatch on the
    --   operation's template with `fromInterface`, and store the given list,
    --   which is the pending list without this operation:
    --
    --   > apply op pending' = case fromInterface @LimitChange op of
    --   >   Some lc -> toInterfaceContractId <$>
    --   >     create this with limit = lc.newLimit, pending = pending'
    --   >   None -> failWithStatus eUnknownOperation
    --
    --   `Timelock_Apply` has already checked that the operation is pending
    --   and ready, and it archives the operation after this method returns.

  unschedule : Pending -> Update (ContractId Timelock)
    -- ^ Create the successor with the given list, which is the pending list
    --   without the cancelled or expired operation:
    --
    --   > unschedule pending' = toInterfaceContractId <$> create this with pending = pending'

  -- Apply a pending operation. Exercised by `Operation_Execute`, which has
  -- verified the executor and the schedule; this choice verifies the pending
  -- list, calls `apply`, and checks that the successor dropped the operation.
  choice Timelock_Apply : ContractId Timelock
    with
      op : ContractId Operation
    controller (view this).authority
    do
      pending' <- takePending op (view this).pending
      opValue <- fetch op
      new <- apply this opValue pending'
      requireDropped op new
      pure new

  -- Remove a pending operation without applying it. Exercised by
  -- `Operation_Cancel` and `Operation_Cleanup`.
  choice Timelock_Drop : ContractId Timelock
    with
      op : ContractId Operation
    controller (view this).authority
    do
      pending' <- takePending op (view this).pending
      new <- unschedule this pending'
      requireDropped op new
      pure new

-- | A scheduled operation with its lifecycle choices. Implement it beside
-- `Timelocked` on every operation template. The template's fields are the
-- operation's typed parameters; the protected contract's `apply` reads them.
--
-- Signatories and observers: those of the implementing template. An operation
--   created in a choice on the protected contract carries `authority`'s
--   signature; executors and cancellers observe it or receive it by
--   disclosure.
-- Controllers: the `actor` argument of each choice. `Operation_Execute`
--   accepts an actor the view lists as executor, or any actor when
--   `executors` is `None`; `Operation_Cancel` accepts an actor the view lists
--   as canceller; `Operation_Cleanup` accepts any actor once the operation has
--   expired.
-- Choices: `Operation_Execute`, `Operation_Cancel`, `Operation_Cleanup`. Each
--   exercises the matching choice on the protected contract and archives the
--   operation.
-- Disclosure and privacy expectations: a pending operation is visible to the
--   stakeholders of the implementing template. An actor outside that set
--   receives the operation and the protected contract by explicit disclosure,
--   and sees both payloads.
-- Authorization assumptions: the choice bodies carry the implementing
--   template's signatures, which is what lets them exercise `Timelock_Apply`
--   and `Timelock_Drop` under `authority`. An operation signed by another
--   party fails that authorization.
-- Archival behavior: each choice archives the operation after the protected
--   contract's choice succeeds.
-- Failure modes: `eNotExecutor`, `eNotCanceller`, and the guards' statuses,
--   plus those of `Timelock_Apply` and `Timelock_Drop`.
-- Upgrade and migration assumptions: frozen for the life of the package. The
--   implementing template upgrades independently.
interface Operation requires Timelocked where
  viewtype OperationView

  -- Execute the operation on `target` once it is ready.
  nonconsuming choice Operation_Execute : ContractId Timelock
    with
      actor : Party
      target : ContractId Timelock
    controller actor
    do
      requireExecutor actor (view this)
      requireReady this
      new <- exercise target Timelock_Apply with op = self
      archive self
      pure new

  -- Withdraw the operation before it executes.
  nonconsuming choice Operation_Cancel : ContractId Timelock
    with
      actor : Party
      target : ContractId Timelock
    controller actor
    do
      requireCanceller actor (view this)
      new <- exercise target Timelock_Drop with op = self
      archive self
      pure new

  -- Remove the operation once it has expired. Open to any actor.
  nonconsuming choice Operation_Cleanup : ContractId Timelock
    with
      actor : Party
      target : ContractId Timelock
    controller actor
    do
      requireExpired this
      new <- exercise target Timelock_Drop with op = self
      archive self
      pure new

-- | Fail with `eNotExecutor` unless `actor` may execute under the view.
requireExecutor : Party -> OperationView -> Update ()
requireExecutor actor v =
  unless (optional True (actor `elem`) v.executors) (failWithStatus eNotExecutor)

-- | Fail with `eNotCanceller` unless `actor` may cancel under the view.
requireCanceller : Party -> OperationView -> Update ()
requireCanceller actor v =
  unless (actor `elem` v.cancellers) (failWithStatus eNotCanceller)

-- Fail with `eOperationRetained` when the successor still lists the operation.
requireDropped : ContractId Operation -> ContractId Timelock -> Update ()
requireDropped op new = do
  successor <- fetch new
  when (toInterfaceContractId @Timelocked op `elem` (view successor).pending)
    (failWithStatus eOperationRetained)

-- Failure statuses. Each is a `FailureStatus` raised with `failWithStatus`, so
-- a rejected submission reaches the Ledger API as `DAML_FAILURE` with the
-- `errorId` below, and a client matches on the id rather than on the message
-- text. In Daml Script, `trySubmit` returns it as `FailureStatusError`.
--
-- Categories follow Canton's error-category semantics for the request itself:
-- `InvalidIndependentOfSystemState` for a request that fails on its own
-- content (an invalid policy, a delay below the minimum), and
-- `InvalidGivenCurrentSystemStateOther` for a request whose outcome depends
-- on ledger time. Waiting turns `eNotReady` into success. `eExpired` and
-- `eNotExpired` share the category because they are the same time comparison;
-- a client treats `eExpired` as final.

-- | Raised when `isValidConfig` rejects a policy: a negative `minDelay`, or a
-- `gracePeriod` of zero or less.
-- `errorId`: `openzeppelin.com/timelock-invalid-config`.
eInvalidConfig : FailureStatus
eInvalidConfig = timelockFailure "invalid-config" InvalidIndependentOfSystemState
  "Timelock: the policy needs a minDelay of zero or greater and a positive gracePeriod"

-- | Raised when an apply, cancel, or cleanup choice presents an operation that
-- the protected contract's `Pending` list does not hold: an operation created
-- outside the schedule choice, one already applied or cancelled, or one
-- scheduled on another protected contract.
-- `errorId`: `openzeppelin.com/timelock-not-pending`.
eNotPending : FailureStatus
eNotPending = timelockFailure "not-pending" InvalidGivenCurrentSystemStateOther
  "Timelock: the operation is not pending on this contract"

-- | Raised when `Operation_Execute`'s actor is outside the view's executors.
-- `errorId`: `openzeppelin.com/timelock-not-executor`.
eNotExecutor : FailureStatus
eNotExecutor = timelockFailure "not-executor" InvalidIndependentOfSystemState
  "Timelock: the actor is not an executor of this operation"

-- | Raised when `Operation_Cancel`'s actor is outside the view's cancellers.
-- `errorId`: `openzeppelin.com/timelock-not-canceller`.
eNotCanceller : FailureStatus
eNotCanceller = timelockFailure "not-canceller" InvalidIndependentOfSystemState
  "Timelock: the actor is not a canceller of this operation"

-- | Raised when `apply` or `unschedule` returns a successor whose pending list still
-- holds the operation, which would let it execute twice.
-- `errorId`: `openzeppelin.com/timelock-operation-retained`.
eOperationRetained : FailureStatus
eOperationRetained = timelockFailure "operation-retained" InvalidIndependentOfSystemState
  "Timelock: the successor still lists the operation as pending"

-- | Raised when a schedule requests a delay shorter than `minDelay`.
-- `errorId`: `openzeppelin.com/timelock-delay-too-short`.
eDelayTooShort : FailureStatus
eDelayTooShort = timelockFailure "delay-too-short" InvalidIndependentOfSystemState
  "Timelock: the delay is shorter than the minimum delay"

-- | Raised when an operation executes before `readyAt`.
-- `errorId`: `openzeppelin.com/timelock-not-ready`.
eNotReady : FailureStatus
eNotReady = timelockFailure "not-ready" InvalidGivenCurrentSystemStateOther
  "Timelock: the operation is not ready"

-- | Raised when an operation executes at or after `expiresAt`.
-- `errorId`: `openzeppelin.com/timelock-expired`.
eExpired : FailureStatus
eExpired = timelockFailure "expired" InvalidGivenCurrentSystemStateOther
  "Timelock: the operation has expired"

-- | Raised when a cleanup runs before `expiresAt`, or on an operation without one.
-- `errorId`: `openzeppelin.com/timelock-not-expired`.
eNotExpired : FailureStatus
eNotExpired = timelockFailure "not-expired" InvalidGivenCurrentSystemStateOther
  "Timelock: the operation has not expired"

timelockFailure : Text -> FailureCategory -> Text -> FailureStatus
timelockFailure name category message = FailureStatus with
  errorId = "openzeppelin.com/timelock-" <> name
  category
  message
  meta = TextMap.empty
